Here's a full rundown of every change made, grouped by category:

Primary crash fix

SoapySDR.Device.enumerate() returns SoapySDRKwargs objects. These are map-like but don't implement the dict API. The fix is dict(results[0]).get("uri", "unknown") — cast to a real dict before calling .get(). The original SoapySDRKwargs object is still passed unmodified to SoapySDR.Device() because that constructor requires it.

Memory leaks

    _all_anomalies and _towers in CollectionSession now cap at MAX_ANOMALIES (10K) and MAX_TOWERS (50K). Both were previously unbounded — a multi-hour session on a dense urban scan could exhaust RAM. Disk output is always complete; only the in-memory summary is capped.
    read_samples() now caps num_samples at MAX_SAMPLE_BUFFER (30M samples = ~240 MB for complex64). A 30-second dwell on a 30 MSPS rate would have allocated 7.2 GB.
    FCCTowerDatabase._load() now checks file size before reading (MAX_DB_BYTES = 500 MB) and caps row count at MAX_TOWERS.

Injection attack surface

    Subprocess args: Both _run_grgsm_decode() and _run_srsran() now build their command lists exclusively from Python hardware constants (int(GSM_SAMPLE_RATE), int(freq_hz), int(bw_hz/1e6)). None of these ever touch RF-sourced data. shell=False is explicit on every subprocess.run() call.
    Subprocess environment: Every subprocess.run() call passes env=SAFE_ENV — a minimal dict with only PATH, HOME, LANG, LC_ALL. Inheriting the full process environment exposes LD_PRELOAD, PYTHONPATH, and other controllable variables.
    CSV formula injection: _sanitize_csv_field() strips any leading =, +, -, @, \t, \r, \n characters from every field before writing. These are the characters that trigger formula execution in spreadsheet apps. All tower CSV fields go through this before DictWriter.writerow().
    RF-sourced string validation: All decoded values from subprocess output now pass through _validate_mcc(), _validate_mnc(), and _validate_int_field() with explicit ranges before entering any dict. MCC/MNC must match ^\d{2,3}$. PCI is clamped to 0–503 (3GPP TS 36.211). TAC 0–65535. ECI 0–268435455. rp_flags is now stored as {"reselection": bool, "path_loss": bool} — not as the raw RF object.
    subprocess output cap: proc.stdout[:MAX_SUBPROCESS_OUTPUT_BYTES] (512 KB) applied before decoding. A rogue signal source emitting garbage data to overflow memory via grgsm output is now bounded.
    _safe_json_loads(): Size-checks each line (MAX_JSON_LINE_BYTES = 64 KB) before calling json.loads(). Prevents a single oversized line from triggering pathological parser behavior.

Temp file hardening

Old: /tmp/ctw11_gsm_{int(time.time())}.cfile — predictable name, world-writable directory, no exclusive create.

New: _make_temp_iq_path() creates /tmp/ctw11_iq/ with mode=0o700, verifies it's not group/world writable (symlink-swap guard), and generates filenames with secrets.token_hex(16). Files are opened with O_CREAT | O_EXCL | 0o600.

File handle safety

CollectionSession.__init__() now has a try/except around the two open() calls. If the second one fails, the first handle is explicitly closed before re-raising. Previously, an OSError on the anomaly file would leave the raw file handle permanently open. Output files are created with O_EXCL so the process never silently overwrites an existing session file.

PlutoSDRDevice.open() now catches exceptions mid-initialization and sets self.sdr = None before re-raising, so __exit__ doesn't attempt to double-free a partially initialized device.

PlutoSDRDevice.close() is now fully idempotent — each SDR call is wrapped in its own try/except so a failure on deactivateStream doesn't block closeStream.

CLI input validation

args.duration clamped to 1–86400 seconds. args.dwell clamped to 0.1–30 seconds. args.uri validated against ^[\w\.\:\-]{1,64}$ before use. args.output run through os.path.realpath() and checked for .. components. All of this happens before any hardware is touched.
